          "text": "{\"snapshotId\":\"capture-af7345b0-33d0-4a72-948b-cd10a12d94fd\",\"url\":\"https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/deprecations.html\",\"title\":\"Deprecated APIs | Node.js v24.21.0 Documentation\",\"capturedAt\":\"2026-10-10T02:48:41.812Z\",\"text\":\"[characters 266-356]\\nDocumentation-only\\nApplication (non-node_modules code only)\\nRuntime (all code)\\nEnd-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 358-842]\\nA Documentation-only deprecation is one that is expressed only within the Node.js API docs. These generate no side-effects while running Node.js. Some Documentation-only deprecations trigger a runtime warning when launched with --pending-deprecation flag (or its alternative, NODE_PENDING_DEPRECATION=1 environment variable), similarly to Runtime deprecations below. Documentation-only deprecations that support that flag are explicitly labeled as such in the list of Deprecated APIs.\\n\\n[characters 844-1277]\\nAn Application deprecation for only non-node_modules code will, by default, generate a process warning that will be printed to stderr the first time the deprecated API is used in code that's not loaded from node_modules. When the --throw-deprecation command-line flag is used, a Runtime deprecation will cause an error to be thrown. When --pending-deprecation is used, warnings will also be emitted for code loaded from node_modules.\\n\\n[characters 1279-1449]\\nA runtime deprecation for all code is similar to the runtime deprecation for non-node_modules code, except that it also emits a warning for code loaded from node_modules.\\n\\n[characters 1783-1864]\\nList of deprecated APIs\\n#\\nDEP0001: http.OutgoingMessage.prototype.flush\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 1866-1883]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 1885-1990]\\nOutgoingMessage.prototype.flush() has been removed. Use OutgoingMessage.prototype.flushHeaders() instead.\\n\\n[characters 1992-2031]\\nDEP0002: require('_linklist')\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 2033-2050]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 2124-2164]\\nDEP0003: _writableState.buffer\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 2166-2183]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 2270-2322]\\nDEP0004: CryptoStream.prototype.readyState\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 2324-2341]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 2343-2402]\\nThe CryptoStream.prototype.readyState property was removed.\\n\\n[characters 2404-2443]\\nDEP0005: Buffer() constructor\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 2445-2491]\\nType: Application (non-node_modules code only)\\n\\n[characters 3226-3497]\\nWithout --pending-deprecation, runtime warnings occur only for code not in node_modules. This means there will not be deprecation warnings for Buffer() usage in dependencies. With --pending-deprecation, a runtime warning results no matter where the Buffer() usage occurs.\\n\\n[characters 3499-3549]\\nDEP0006: child_process options.customFds\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 3551-3568]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 3570-3733]\\nWithin the child_process module's spawn(), fork(), and exec() methods, the options.customFds option is deprecated. The options.stdio option should be used instead.\\n\\n[characters 3735-3818]\\nDEP0007: Replace cluster worker.suicide with worker.exitedAfterDisconnect\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 3820-3837]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 3839-4277]\\nIn an earlier version of the Node.js cluster, a boolean property with the name suicide was added to the Worker object. The intent of this property was to provide an indication of how and why the Worker instance exited. In Node.js 6.0.0, the old property was deprecated and replaced with a new worker.exitedAfterDisconnect property. The old property name did not precisely describe the actual semantics and was unnecessarily emotion-laden.\\n\\n[characters 4279-4323]\\nDEP0008: require('node:constants')\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 4325-4349]\\nType: Documentation-only\\n\\n[characters 4641-4688]\\nDEP0009: crypto.pbkdf2 without digest\\n#\\nHistory\\n\\n[characters 4690-4707]\\nType: End-of-Life\\n\\n[characters 4709-5044]\\nUse of the crypto.pbkdf2() API without specifying a digest was deprecated in Node.js 6.0 because the method defaulted to using the non-recommended 'SHA1' digest. Previously, a deprecatio\n\n[... tool result middle pruned ...]\n\n234,\"text\":\"DEP0015: fs.readSync legacy String interface\\n#\\nHistory\"},{\"charStart\":6236,\"charEnd\":6253,\"text\":\"Type: End-of-Life\"},{\"charStart\":6375,\"charEnd\":6405,\"text\":\"DEP0016: GLOBAL/root\\n#\\nHistory\"},{\"charStart\":6407,\"charEnd\":6424,\"text\":\"Type: End-of-Life\"},{\"charStart\":6541,\"charEnd\":6580,\"text\":\"DEP0017: Intl.v8BreakIterator\\n#\\nHistory\"},{\"charStart\":6582,\"charEnd\":6599,\"text\":\"Type: End-of-Life\"},{\"charStart\":6694,\"charEnd\":6741,\"text\":\"DEP0018: Unhandled promise rejections\\n#\\nHistory\"},{\"charStart\":6743,\"charEnd\":6760,\"text\":\"Type: End-of-Life\"},{\"charStart\":6762,\"charEnd\":6886,\"text\":\"Unhandled promise rejections are deprecated. By default, promise rejections that are not handled terminate the Node.js proce\"}],\"sourcePath\":\"C:\\\\Temp\\\\weftmate-fact1-9fdeb337-f0f5-4f2f-8446-7da42141df40\\\\profile\\\\conversations\\\\68660e8fd739c36111f8ef8ffd451ef2fcf59726e93f41a61e38e92b3b0b751d\\\\session-b75589f2-2dd3-47de-9e72-3945321ae904\\\\.weftmate-web-sources\\\\capture-af7345b0-33d0-4a72-948b-cd10a12d94fd.txt\"}"
